1. Clear DNS Cache:
   
 When you access a website, your computer uses a cached DNS entry to resolve the website’s domain name to its IP address. If the website’s IP address changes, and your system is still using the old DNS information, it won’t connect to the correct server. This is where clearing the DNS cache can help.


 - cmd => ipconfig /flushdns
   linux => systemd-resolved is a DNS service used on many modern Linux distributions, including Ubuntu.
             dnsmasq is used on some lightweight or custom Linux systems for DNS caching.
             BIND is often used as a DNS server on enterprise or server systems.
             nscd is sometimes used to cache DNS queries.
             Unbound is another DNS resolver used in some systems.

             for systemd-resolved => sudo systemd-resolve --flush-caches
             for dnsmasq  =>sudo systemctl restart dnsmasq
             for BIND => sudo rndc flush
             FOR NSCD => sudo systemctl restart nscd

